  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA mid level short wave will move east across the mid Ohio Valley \ntonight as a frontal boundary sags slowly south across the region.  \nThe airmass across our area has begun to slowly recover with MLCapes\nnow back up to around 1000 J/KG. This will allow for showers and \nsome embedded thunderstorms to overspread our area through the rest \nof the afternoon and into this evening. Will likely see some \nadditional showers then linger through much of the night across \nmainly our southeast as the boundary continues to push slowly south.\n\n\nThe overall severe threat will be limited by the marginal \ninstability, but increasing mid level flow through late afternoon \ncould allow for a few stronger to possibly severe storms if we are \nable to destabilize a bit more. The main threat would be gusty winds\ninto early this evening. \n\nPWs ahead of the front will remain in excess of 2 inches tonight. \nThus, locally heavy rainfall will remain possible along with \nthe potential for flooding in areas where storms are able to train. \nThe best chance for this appears to be across southeast portions of \nour area into tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nThe frontal boundary will push slowly south on Friday before lifting\nback to the north on Saturday ahead of an approaching mid level \nshort wave. This will lead to another chance of showers and \nthunderstorms for the first half of the weekend.",
